seaweed is washing up all over the  beaches of southern Spain it's an  eyesore but it also threatens an ancient  way of life  Alma drama is a fishing technique dating  back more than 3,000 years nets are  positioned in sections of the sea close  to the Strait of Gibraltar to catch tuna  as they migrate from the Atlantic to the  Mediterranean in recent years fishermen  say their nets have been clogged up with  nothing but seaweed seaweed is  proliferating in this area as never  before I've been fishing for 35 years  and it's become a huge problem it's  causing great economic hardship and it's  endangering I'm a driver fishing attack  fishermen cast the nets in March and  pull them up in June they say the  invasive seaweed is scaring away the  fish it sticks to the net and then grows  creating an underwater wall the weight  and the tension breaks the Nets also  tuna is a fish that likes clear water so  when it sees darkness it swims away the  seaweed causing the problems originates  in Japan scientists have been studying  how it ended up in the waters around  Spain the seaweed may have come from the  Marseille area from a lagoon installed  in 2002 to facilitate the cultivation of  Japanese oysters ships probably carried  it from there to here all this coincided  with the highest temperature recorded in  the south of the Iberian Peninsula in  2015 so the stars aligned in this  species has increased dramatically  scientists are developing a coating that  could be applied to nets to stop seaweed  attaching to them for fishermen who  didn't catch their quota of tuna last  year and won't this year a solution  can't come soon enough  Victoria Gaytan B al-jazeera
 

